      "id": "thm-l-two-kernels-give-hilbert-schmidt-operators",
      "location": "step 4.1 (Measurability)",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 4.1 asserts: 'the function g_j(x,y):=k_0(x,y)1_{B_j}(y) satisfies ∫|g_j| dρ <= ||k_0||_{L2(ρ)} ν(B_j)^{1/2} < +∞ by Cauchy–Schwarz in L2(ρ)', and then invokes Fubini [F4] on that basis. Cauchy–Schwarz in L2(ρ) with the indicator of X×B_j gives only ∫|g_j| dρ <= ||k_0||_{L2(ρ)}·ρ(X×B_j)^{1/2} = ||k_0||_{L2(ρ)}·(μ(X)ν(B_j))^{1/2}; the factor μ(X)^{1/2} is missing, so the displayed bound is false whenever μ(X) > 1. Counterexample within the hypotheses: X = Y = {0,1} with counting measure, k_0 ≡ 1 (so ||k_0||_{L2(ρ)} = 2), B_j = Y (ν(B_j) = 2): the left side is ρ(X×Y) = 4, the written right side is 2·2^{1/2} ≈ 2.83. The invoked Fubini hypothesis genuinely fails in general: on X = Y = N with counting measure take k_0(m,n) = 1/m for n = 0 and 0 otherwise, so ||k_0||^2_{L2(ρ)} = Σ_{m>=1} 1/m^2 < ∞, and take the finite-measure simple f = 1_{{0}} (ν({0}) = 1); then ∫|g_j| dρ = Σ_{m>=1} 1/m = ∞, so g_j is not in L1(ρ) and [F4] (Fubini for L1) does not apply. The conclusion of the step (μ-measurability and μ-a.e. finiteness of the section-integral function) is true and recoverable from Tonelli's measurability of the section integrals together with the a.e. finiteness supplied by steps 2.1/3.1, but the written inequality and the L1-based justification are defective as they stand.",
